# Automerge credentials. Copy to `.env` and fill in; `.env` is git-ignored.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Secrets and nothing else. Everything the bot decides -- which repository it
# merges, which branches, what has to be approved, the merge strategy -- lives in
# `automerge.yaml`, versioned and reviewed like any other code.
#
# Use ONE of the two options below. The bot merges and comments as whoever owns
# these credentials, so the account needs write access to the repository, and it
# must NOT be an account whose approval is expected to count (the author of a
# pull request never covers its own files).

# -- Option 1: repository or workspace access token ---------------------------
# Bitbucket: Repository settings > Security > Access tokens > Create.
# Scopes: Pull requests: Write, Pipelines: Read, Repositories: Read.
# Paste the raw value only -- no "Bearer", no quotes. The bot builds the
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header itself.
# Comments show up as the token's name, with a generated avatar that cannot be
# replaced. Use option 2 if the bot should have a face.
AUTOMERGE_BITBUCKET_TOKEN=

# -- Option 2: user + app password --------------------------------------------
# A dedicated Atlassian account (for example automerge@example.com) invited to the
# workspace. Its display name and profile picture are what every comment shows;
# both are set in the Atlassian profile, there is no API for the avatar.
# Bitbucket: Personal settings > App passwords > Create.
# Permissions: Pull requests: Write, Pipelines: Read, Repositories: Read.
AUTOMERGE_BITBUCKET_USERNAME=
AUTOMERGE_BITBUCKET_APP_PASSWORD=
